# thread-split.allow — the files that may name engine memory, each with its class, its
# scope and the argument for it (tools/thread-split-check.sh; frame-packet-exchange §10).
#
# DEFAULT-DENY: only a file listed here may name an engine address, include the engine
# header, probe with IsBad*Ptr or add an offset to the main pointer, with comments
# stripped. The list only SHRINKS: a change that converts a file to the packet removes
# its line in the same diff. Adding a
# line loosens the rule for every future edit of that file and is the owner's decision,
# taken in a review — never a session's way past a red build.
#
# Classes, one per line after the path:
#   publisher         game-thread code inside an engine call, at a site that owns the
#                     memory it reads (observers, the packet's fill, detour installers
#                     that byte-match at attach)
#   session-reader    render thread, data that lives for the session and is never
#                     rewritten (GAF banks, the cursor table); NOT fonts
#   fenced            render thread, per-LEVEL assets (model templates, the tile set)
#                     under tagpu_reclaim's teardown fence; retired by the asset channel,
#                     the decision after landing 5
#   tooling           trigger-gated readers (peek, catalogues, the GUI snapshot, the
#                     weapon dump, scenario detection); out of the exchange's scope.
#                     THEY RUN ON THE GAME THREAD, from the engine's own flip
#                     (tagpu_gui_hook.c's before_flip) rather than from
#                     tagpu_overlay_draw -- which is what lets them reach the GDI backend
#                     -- so they read engine memory from the thread that owns it.
#   pure-engine-code  render thread executes an engine FUNCTION over our buffers, with
#                     the disassembly range that shows it reads only its arguments and
#                     writes only its destination
#   engine-map        the header the addresses live in (publisher files include it)
#   to-convert:N      render-thread reader of per-frame or per-map engine state, to be
#                     converted in landing N of the migration table
#
# Scope, one word after the class (tools/sim-state-check.sh; inc/tagpu_simreg.h):
#   sim               the simulation's own code: every writable static, heap table and TLS
#                     slot it has is declared to the demo recorder's registry, and its memory,
#                     TLS indices, page changes and levers come through the registry's
#                     wrappers. A source file only: a header's statics belong to the objects
#                     that include it.
#   presentation      everything else: renderers, the UI, the camera, the menu, reclamation,
#                     scenarios and catalogues, the TADR takeover. Out of the recorder's
#                     scope, so marking a file that holds simulation state `presentation` is
#                     a loosening, and the owner's decision.
#
# The argument after `#` is what the reviewer checks, not what the script checks.

inc/tagpu_engine.h       engine-map          presentation  # the addresses, for publisher files only
src/tagpu_packet_pub.c   publisher           presentation  # the frame packet's fill, from DrawGameScreen's `after` at 0x4969D2; the font copy at hook 8; the level-end packet; and LoadMap's join 0x483B53 on the LOADER thread (mapfeat_at_load): LoadMap's own frame, the FeatureDefs 0x421F20 has just counted and the grid's heights LoadMap has just written, read after those writes in LoadMap's own order on its own thread, before the level's first in-play draw; the list it builds is ours, and reaches the game thread only through the interlocked hand-over slot
src/tagpu_markown.c      publisher           presentation  # the marker block's call-site redirects on the game thread; hook 8 latches the font through the publisher
src/tagpu_menu.c         publisher           presentation  # the render-options screen: GUI calls on the game thread from the DrawGameScreen observer, and the teardown wrap's entry (0x491B60, on the game thread) lets its screen slot go: it writes the saved name back into main+0x37EA0 and forgets its screen without a pop; for the replay HUD's modal test and Leave, on the game thread, it reads main+0x37EBE (u16) and main+0x2BEE (u8) and walks the GUI chain from the top screen down its +0 links, bounded at 32, reading each screen's record-0 name; and from the DrawGameScreen observer, for the replay HUD's Leave, it compares 0x460680..0x4607F1 and 0x4605C0..0x46065C by FNV-1a with the retail exe's bytes, writes 0x512FF8 = 0 and calls the YESORNO opener 0x460680 and SETLABEL 0x4A07D0 on the top screen; and for the in-game wiki (tagpu_wiki.c), on the game thread, it reads main+0x2A44 bit 0 (a network game) and read-modify-writes bit 0 of main+0x38A51 (single player's pause), the write only in a single-player level and only at the wiki's open and close -- the owner's decision of 2026-10-25 (ingame-wiki-plan.md, question 2)
src/tagpu_replays.c      publisher           presentation  # the shell's Replays screen and the main menu's REPLAYS button, on the game thread inside the engine's shell calls: GUI_Load 0x4AACAE writes the main menu's parsed records before its stage 1, the PCX loader's return 0x429290 the FrontendX surface it has just decoded before its first copy (and, for one of our own grounds, only that surface's size and pixel pointer), the builder 0x42644C the screen's OnCommand slot it has just written, and the list draw 0x4A1B40 draws into our own screen's panel surface; the OnCommands are the engine calling us from its pump. One read is on the LOADER thread: the unit-data load 0x42D2E0's entry takes UNITINFOCount, which the game thread reads at the level's first step, after the load. For the in-game wiki's asset loader, on the game thread when the load starts (tagpu_wiki.c: the wiki's first open, or the parity check's trigger, tagpu_wiki.check, with the window closed), it copies the mount list's paths -- the list running_mod reads, the graphics globals' [+0x618] bounded by [+0x61C] and 0x104 bytes a path -- with the `language` value 0x51FB50 and the asset folders' names in .data (0x503920, 0x503720, 0x504734, 0x503704, 0x5036E4, 0x503740, 0x502E30, 0x502C7C, and for the wiki's sidebar 0x502820, 0x503360, 0x505FE4, 0x503730, 0x50925C, 0x5031BC, 0x5033A4, 0x5031C8) -- the language written once by WinMain's init, the names passed by the engine only as paths [INFERRED], some rewritten by a mod's loader DLL in DllMain before the engine's entry point (Mayhem, ProTA: exe-reverse-engineering.md, The left sidebar) -- each bounded to its buffer, and hands them to the loader by value: the loader reads no engine memory. It holds no simulation state
src/tagpu_hud.c          publisher           presentation  # HUD scale: an observer on the loading-screen flip 0x4288D0 gated on the return address 0x498242 writes the viewport rect's R/B/W/H on the game thread; the one word it publishes is its own
src/tagpu_reclaim.c      publisher           presentation  # FreeObjectState and the teardown wrap on the game thread; the fence itself
src/tagpu_gui_hook.c     publisher           presentation  # the UI observers and the flip publisher on the game thread; the probes are on its own arena; the flip marker takes the Config panel's box from tagpu_menu.c, Impure's own state
src/tagpu_gui_leaves.h   publisher           presentation  # observers on the game thread, all of them. The minimap picture main+0x1426B is alive for the whole level, not only inside BuildMinimapSurface 0x466780, so the packet's publisher decodes it on the level's first in-play draw. Nothing in this file runs on the loader thread
src/tagpu_order.c        publisher           presentation  # the order-marker snapshot at 0x469BFC, game thread: it resolves every unit pointer to an array SLOT and every def and weapon field to a number in the record, so the present half reads the packet's units and GUI colours and dereferences nothing. The cursor art is decoded on the game thread into an atlas of ours, written once and published by a release store (art_build)
src/tagpu_owndraw.c      publisher           presentation  # the composite detours' classifier on the game thread (0x459830/0x459C70)
src/tagpu_fxown.c        publisher           presentation  # effects call-site redirects and leaf detours on the game thread
src/tagpu_featown.c      publisher           presentation  # the feature leaf detour 0x46A610 on the game thread
src/tagpu_terrown.c      publisher           presentation  # the terrain and fog-overlay detours; the fog grid's lazy rebuild on the game thread
src/tagpu_fogwide.c      publisher           presentation  # the wide fog grid: built on the GAME thread inside an in-play draw — at the engine's own fog site when it is ours, else, on the Vulkan renderer, in the publisher's `after` of the same draw — after the level load that allocates the LOS and MAPPED maps and before the teardown (which frees MAPPED; the LOS counters are freed by the next load). It hands over nothing: the packet's publisher copies the grid out of it on this thread, in the same draw, so it needs no hand-over buffer, critical section or retire ring
src/tagpu_patches.c      publisher           sim           # byte patches applied at attach, before the engine runs; and the terrain window bound, which reads the pass's frame, the tile map, the tile set and the OFFSCREEN on every in-play draw, on the game thread, inside 0x483FA0 through its 0x484057 stub; and, not the game thread, the saved-feature restore on the LOADER thread: it reads and writes the feature grid's def words and refreshes the pathing maps around 0x424C00's one call at 0x43265A, and reads the grid and the FeatureDefs where 0x424C00 writes a saved record's state (0x4250C0, 0x425185) -- inside the level load, in the engine's own order on its own thread, before any unit is restored and before the level's first in-play draw; and at a seek on a level that plays a recording (tagpu_replay.c), on the game thread in the frame visit between two sim steps: tagpu_effects_clear destroys every particle object of the 10 layers at *(main+0x38D77) through its own destructor and empties the layer vectors, as the teardown 0x471DE0's first loop does, then zeroes the explosion count and re-initialises the debris records and the flying-piece slots (the raised build's own pools, or the stock build's at main+0x1491B, main+0x1AB9F with their blocks, and 0x511DF0), and, at that seek and at every capture point and frame visit of such a level, tagpu_patches_restored bounds the air pool under its lock (held from tagpu_patches_restore_begin over the replay's table write, C code alone), the hit delay queue and the ghost sweep's count; the render thread reads none of it; and the step's phases, two stubs on the step's calls of the projectile tick (0x495513) and the explosion tick (0x495539) on the GAME thread, which call the recorder's and the replay's phase functions around the engine's own call and read nothing themselves; and the script slice: a stub on the unit tick's script call (0x48ADDF..0x48ADF0) on the GAME thread inside the unit tick. It reads the COB object +0x9A of the unit the engine's loop is on, as the engine's own code there does, calls the recorder's turn and marker and the replay's deferral, and calls DoScriptsNow 0x4B0D60 itself, for that unit and for a playing's deferred slices
src/tagpu_suppress.c     publisher           presentation  # the DrawUnit detour's classifier on the game thread
src/tagpu_tracer.c       publisher           presentation  # the draw tracer's detours on the game thread
src/tagpu_cobtrace.c     publisher           sim           # the COB oracle's five sites inside the script engine, game thread
src/tagpu_datakeys.c     publisher           sim           # TADR section C's unit keys. Observers at the unit-data load's start 0x42D2E0, the FBI loader's entry 0x42BF40 and its read site 0x42BF97, and the COB checksum 0x4B6BA0, on the LOADER thread (the level's unit-data load) and on the GAME thread (the console's one-type reload 0x42D1F0): the section the loader holds, through the engine's own reader 0x4C48C0, and each script block's address and length; the records they write are ours. The ghost masks, on the GAME thread inside the in-play packet fill: the def array, MODEL_PTRS, each type's COB (def+0x18E) and template tree, all of the LEVEL, loaded before its first in-play draw and freed by its teardown on this thread, the COB also by a Reload on this thread; every read of a COB is bounded by its recorded length. The weapon keys (C2): their store written on the LOADER thread (emptied at 0x42E310, filled at 0x42E468) with a weapon's +0x111 noradar bit at 0x49E010, and read on the GAME thread by the targeting sites, which clear a slot's target through the engine's ClearTarget 0x48A0F0; nomapweaponalert's hit frame, blink hold and the save record's byte +0xB3 on the GAME thread (the sim tick, the dispatcher's 0x0B case, the unit saver), the hold restored on the LOADER thread inside the same load that emptied it. The render thread reads none of it: the masks reach it as a packet table
src/tagpu_weapons.c      publisher           sim           # the extra-weapons module: sim-side sites on the game thread; its def records written on the LOADER thread inside the unit-data load (emptied at 0x42D2E0's entry, filled at 0x42CEF2), before the level's first in-play tick, and at a console Reload on the game thread (the piece caches forgotten at 0x42BF40's entry, the whole record rewritten at 0x42CEF2 when the FBI opens); the WEAPON_FIRED receiver can also run during a load (the dispatcher 0x453D40 at 0x49852E and 0x49727D), reads those records bounded and can fill one piece cache, in an array never freed; at each capture point, frame visit and seek of a level that plays a recording, on the game thread between two sim steps, tagpu_weapons_restored bounds each def record def_rec can return (its count against its weapons and masks, its piece caches) after the rebuild has written them; the weapon dump is tooling
src/tagpu_recorder.c     publisher           presentation  # the demo recorder's capture, inside B2's stub at the sim step's unit-tick call 0x4954ED, on the GAME thread between two iterations: the unit array (bounded by the engine's own slot count, checked against its last-slot pointer), a live unit's mover (0x2F bytes the creates allocate and only the destructor frees, nulling +0x00 in the same call) and its two order lists through each node's next (every node made, linked and freed on this thread; a vtable test and a 4096-node cap end the recording rather than read on; a node's owned object read once for its vtable), the ten player records and their PlayerInfo blocks (nothing frees main's), and the declared SIM_STATE rows, each bounded by its declaration; for the header and the harness's dump also the unit-def and weapon names (UNITINFOCount of them, the weapon array's slots), for the header alone each unit def's air bit (+0x241) and its sight-height and five flight fields (+0x170 to +0x1A9, inside the def's 0x249 bytes, the defs below UNITINFOCount) and the level's gravity and sea level (main+0x14263, +0x1427F), the player names and alliance rows, the game options, the skirmish settings' start mode and map name, and GameingState's TNT path; and the features (the slice note's S3a): the feature grid, the wreck pool, their three list heads and the reproduction cursor, and the feature defs below their count, each checked at every capture against the ones the recording began with (the level's own, which only the teardown frees), a cell an argument names bounded by the grid's columns and rows and a record by the pool's count before anything is read there; and the globals and the projectiles (the slice note's S3b): the fields of the main block and .data replay-globals.tsv lists, each inside the main block's 0x3924D bytes or .data by the generator's check, and the projectile pool through main's pool pointer, checked at every capture against the one the recording began with, its count bounded by the pool's TAGPU_LIM_PROJ records (a count past them ends the recording) and its records read below the written extent, which is bounded by the same; and the explored map through main+0x14273, checked at every capture against the one the recording began with and read within the bytes LoadMap computes from the grid's columns and rows (a block only the level's end frees); the perspective of a skirmish's human seat, and in a multiplayer game of each local actor a human controls (published as contributions from the same game thread): the scroll target, the 1x view's size, the world point's integer halves and the region bits, which only game-thread code writes (the mouse thread writes only 0x51F4B6 and 0x51F4D6), and the controlled player's (or that actor's) unit block walked within player_units' bound (first and last on the array's own slots, aligned, at most the units a player may have) reading each unit's +0x110 and its births column row; for the harness's dump also the explored map, each player record's sight counters within the block the player init allocated at the size it stored beside it, and the main block and .data whole, both mapped for the process's life. An id row's pointer is never dereferenced but an order node's owned object: its ID is one more than its row in its table. The event hooks run on the GAME thread inside the engine's own calls (the create's reservation after B4 stamps the selected slot and its successful exit, the destructor's entry, the three script broadcasts, the build-done, the state setter, the two replies, the nano query, emitters and handler calls, thirteen owner-only COB starts, the QueryBuildInfo call 0x4027FB, the fire functions' 0x0D and area damage's 0x0E through tagpu_patches.c, the seven feature functions' and the corpse helper 0x486360's entries and returns, the three footprint writers' entries and, on a level that plays a recording, their returns, the attach 0x48AB70's entry (its record's seven bytes and, on slots bounded by the slot count first, the child's and the parent's +0x110, +0x8A and +0x86), and the feature code's six calls of the simulation RNG, the destructor's explosion call 0x486D50 and the two click actions' only call sites 0x4995B3 (0x498F70) and 0x4993AC (0x499100) and Teleport's set-position call 0x406BC0 (0x48A9F0; it reads the moved unit's +0x6A..+0x75 and +0x110 once the pointer is a slot by arithmetic), redirected to stubs that call the engine's own function (a click reads the handler's stack copy of its message, the armed order, the cursor index, the region bits, the Interface Type and the world point's integer halves, all written by the game thread earlier in the same handler; in a multiplayer game the contributing game thread publishes it at the channel's capture tick), and reproduction's source test 0x424185..0x42418E, redirected to a stub that makes the same test on the engine's own cell pointer (the cursor's cell), or on a multiplayer recording's puppet level takes the recording's value for it): they read the call's own arguments and registers no further than the engine does, name a unit only once its pointer is a slot by arithmetic, and a call on any other thread is counted and not recorded; the five fire functions' network tests are released while recording, which builds the packet on their own stack and runs the extra weapons' slot-index splice, which writes no simulation state (tagpu_weapons.c, cb_slot_index). The recording ends at the teardown wrap's entry, before the cascade frees the array, and does not start without that wrap. Multiplayer constructor/script/reply/spray hooks use a game-thread-owned copy of the validated Level, bound unit/player pointers before reading their COB/owner fields, copy the actual main+0x38A47 tick and exact declared incarnation token, and queue only locally controlled frozen blocks. The constructor reservation reads only the slot's owner/water fields: its owner is assigned by unit-array initialization before any constructor, so it follows no uninitialized COB, definition or mover. The copied queue drains before source seals and its thread ID/context are cleared at that same teardown entry; no engine pointer reaches its worker. Its primary receive entry wrapper runs on the GAME or LOADER pump caller, owns a stack-local frame and a process-lifetime packet allocation reused per recursion depth, uses TLS only for the current exact delivered span, and serializes receive/dispatch under a nonblocking recursive critical section; a busy caller returns without consuming a message, and nested calls restore parent source/destination words in the process-lifetime main block. The replacement calls the original transport or raw SDK receive on that engine caller and writes only the existing dispatcher stack pointer on success. No primary interface or input allocation reaches a worker. It hands images to the writer thread through a ring; the writer, tagpu_recwriter.c, reads no engine memory and is not listed. And the counts, on the GAME thread: count-only observers on 21 effect sinks (CNT_SINKS) that read their return address (the flare 0x472630 called from the explosion add also one word of the add's own frame, [esp+0x20], the add's return address, after a byte match of the add at attach; a count key, never dereferenced), and for the COB thread allocation 0x4B08C0 the script's name as the engine reads it, inside its call on the unit's live COB object (the index bounded by the header's count, the name at most 31 bytes to its NUL). And the script slices: two observers on the COB start entries 0x4B0B00 and 0x4B0940 read ecx, the COB object, as a key, never dereferenced, as the owner's two queries note theirs (the unit's +0x9A, read once the unit is a slot of the array by arithmetic); the slice marker names the unit the unit tick's loop is on by arithmetic, as the recorder's turn from the same slice stub does
src/tagpu_puppet.c       publisher           sim           # the puppet host's table (tagpu_puppet.h): whether this level plays a recording, which records are puppet seats and getter 75's answer for each, SIM_CONTENT (decision D3), written by the seat fill on the GAME thread in the SKIRMISH Start before the loader thread exists, by a live rewind's start on the GAME thread, its getter-75 answers anew for the segment a playing's seek stands in and for a takeover's new seat types (tagpu_puppet_locals, on the GAME thread between two steps inside the seek, after the loader's last read), and cleared by the teardown wrap on the game thread; read by COB's getter on the game thread and the loader thread, and by the loader's start-unit stub, between those two points. And its gates: prologue gates landed at attach on twenty engine functions (the damage apply 0x489CE0, Send_UnitDeath 0x4864B0, COB's SET 0x480B20 at its second instruction 0x480B26, ATTACH 0x481340 and DROP 0x4813B0, the replies 0x47F780 and 0x47F850, the meteor shower 0x437DE0, typed commands 0x417B50, the debug keys 0x4956C0, the resource transfer 0x464C60, the save 0x4326B0, and the feature tick 0x424050, the spawn 0x423C50, the destroy 0x4246B0, FeatureDie 0x423550, the burn 0x4233A0, the damage 0x4244B0 and the swap 0x423710, and the skirmish elimination 0x47BD70, refused only while a seek rebuilds the level). A predicate reads nothing but the mode unless the level plays a recording; then, on the thread the engine calls the function on, it reads the call's own arguments and registers, the unit array's bounds from main (the level holds the array until its teardown, whose wrap ends the mode first), a unit's owner byte only once its pointer is a slot by arithmetic, and a COB object's unit as the function itself reads it. And the footprint writers' undo, from the recorder's observers at the stamp 0x47CC30, the clear 0x47D0E0 and the re-claim 0x47C790 on the GAME thread: on a level that plays a skirmish's recording whose cells are not held (a multiplayer one, and one that holds its cells, leave the writes standing) it saves the unit's footprint cells, the unit a slot by arithmetic first and the rectangle clipped to the grid's W and H from main, and puts their unit slots and yard bit back at the call's return. And the contest's rule: on a level that plays a skirmish recording holding its cells, the six two-byte `je` sites of the re-claim 0x47C790 and the stamp 0x47CC30 (0x47C856, 0x47C95F, 0x47CA3B, 0x47CDD9, 0x47CF0F, 0x47CFE9) written to 90 90 through tagpu_detour_write on the GAME thread at the replay's first step, a seek's crossing or a rewind's start, and put back at the teardown, a takeover's end or a return to now, between two iterations and outside every footprint writer, each site only while it holds the retail bytes or this module's NOPs. And three observers counting COB's engine writes on a puppet (the state setter 0x48B090, 0x47DAC0, 0x48AAC0), on the GAME thread inside the engine's call: the return address, the unit argument as a slot by arithmetic, its owner byte and GameTime from main
src/tagpu_replay.c       publisher           presentation  # playing a recording back -- a skirmish's, or a multiplayer game's through the owner-authority materializer (the units, players and order lists its owners contributed, applied in the step their recorder took them, the death of each through the destructor with the feature gates shut and its record's corpse level cleared, each death's feature calls made from the recorder's stream as roots -- its corpse through 0x486360 on a stack record of the recorded fields it reads (the type bounded by the unit types' count, every corpse def by the feature defs' count) -- and reproduction's source test answered from the recording, and each live unit's model instance's composite frame Object3do+0x10 cleared when its composed image changes whether its build is finished, with main+0x37EBE bit 4 set when that finishes a unit the viewer has selected, on the GAME thread inside the step, the model being the one the host's create made (0x485DCC, 0x485E0E), freed only by its destructor on the same thread) -- on the GAME thread: the SKIRMISH Start's calls of the seat fill (0x47B092) and of the settings persist (0x47B09C), in the Start handler, read and write the ten player records and their PlayerInfo blocks (main's, which nothing frees), the skirmish settings the Start has just filled from, GameingState's mode and the objective the .ota read at 0x47AF77 armed; the Replays browser's Watch makes the same Start on the GAME thread, inside REPLAYS' OnCommand in the pump's dispatch (tagpu_replay_watch): it snapshots the settings block's ten seat entries, its map and main's seat count (main+0x38D81), writes the recording's in their place, and puts the player's back on a refusal, at the teardown, and around each settings save (the observer's exchange); between those it calls the engine's own players reset 0x4644D0, preferences load 0x42F9A0, SetGameMode 0x434AB0, InitTAHPIAry 0x41D4C0, the .ota read 0x435A20, the Start's checks 0x437300 and 0x479760, the seat fill 0x47A760 and 0x41DA30, writes main's player count, front-end bit 3, shell group and shell request, shows the Start's notice 0x4ABD90 and pops every screen through GUI_Pop 0x4A9660, after comparing the engine code it rests on with its retail bytes; the main menu builder's one call 0x4270A9 hands the browser the main menu once after such a level, in the shell tick, reading main 0x512C80; the in-game menu's RESTART name test 0x460880 reads only the puppet flag; the loader's two start-unit calls (0x497963, 0x497ACD) read only the puppet flag, written before the loader thread exists; the allocator observer at 0x48587E runs on the LOADER thread, claims an immutable copied roster through an interlocked state, reads the freshly allocated arena/counts and ten player-record pointer values, and replaces only the validated ten-pointer permutation on its own stack before the native ownership initializer. It publishes READY/FAILED through an interlocked operation; the level init's only call 0x4919C8 runs on the LOADER thread too, in LoadGameData_Main, retargeted to tagpu_replay_level_players: it calls the engine's own 0x464990 and then, only on a level whose seating armed it (an interlocked state written on the game thread before the loader thread exists, with the lowest seat copied beside it), checks record 10's first dword, type and two buffer pointers and calls the player init 0x464700 on it, or on a failure writes main+0x2A42/+0x2A43 to that lowest seat, and publishes READY/FAILED through an interlocked operation, which the first GAME-thread step reads after the engine's load-done gate; the first GAME-thread step reads that result after the engine's load-done gate. Start undo precedes loader creation and teardown follows loader completion, so neither resets the copied plan during its consumption; the step inside B2's stub at the unit-tick call 0x4954ED, between two iterations, reads the level's counts, GameingState's TNT path and GameTime, and writes V's (record 10's) sight counters and its bit in the explored map at the first step or frame visit, then asks for (main+0x142F1 bit 2) and runs the minimap's fog composite 0x466C20 on the images the level init makes (0x4919C3) and only the teardown frees (0x491BAE), each unit's control group only through the engine's setter 0x480250, the group and the unit's current one each bounded by the owner's 10-entry table, the feature grid's cells and the tick's input written back from the next iteration's world, the wreck pool's records, the reproduction cursor within the level's grid, pool and counts, and calls the feature functions with recorded arguments bounded by the grid, the feature defs and the weapons (THE FEATURES); the recorder's stub at the destructor's explosion call 0x486D50 makes those calls for a skirmish recording's death being replayed, inside the destructor; the observer on the settings save 0x430F00 exchanges the settings with the player's around each save on the save's thread, the game thread (no call of it is on the loader thread), and only from the level's first step, when the loader has read them; the teardown wrap's entry puts the settings back, and its post hook, after 0x491B60 returns on the same thread, writes record 10 whole and V's PlayerInfo back and zeroes main+0x2A42/+0x2A43 when either names record 10; the first call of B2's stub, at the capture point, and the frame visit before the in-play draw call 0x4969CD (paused frames included), both between two iterations, write every declared table that pairs with this build's declaration (as the seek does, below), the main block's and .data's fields replay-globals.tsv lists as `write` (each inside the main block or .data by the generator's check), LosType bits 0-2 with the engine's own reaction (every temporary sight record's expiry zeroed, the engine's count of them bounded by the 20 its add allows, then the expiry 0x482130 and the sight rebuild 0x4816A0, then V's sight again with the same composite), each live unit's water state (bounded by its enum) and sight stamp's fields through the removal 0x482090 and the stamp 0x482270 (a circle radius index bounded by the frame table's count) where they differ from the recording's, and the projectile pool through main's pool pointer, its count and extent bounded by the pool's TAGPU_LIM_PROJ records and every pointer formed from an ID within its table (the weapon array, the unit array, the pool), and the explored map within the bytes LoadMap computes from the host's grid, equal to the recording's, with LosType bit 3 cleared when an entry changed, and the frame visit reads and deletes the trigger file and runs a seek: after bounding the recording's world at the target (the feature lists, records and cells, the sort buckets and their chains, the explored map's size, the projectile pool, LosType's slot and the engine's count of temporary sight records), it empties the projectile pool, kills every unit through the destructor (C4's wrap, severity and corpse 0) with the skirmish elimination gated, drops the temporary sight records through 0x482130, resets the sight through 0x4816A0, sets GameTime, makes every recorded unit through the create, runs the step's appliers, sets each unit's water state (bounded by its enum), its state byte through the setter 0x48B090, and its sight stamp's fields through the removal 0x482090 and the stamp 0x482270 (a circle radius index bounded by the frame table's count), moves the units of the recorded buckets through the unlink 0x47CB00 and the move 0x47CB60, writes the wreck pool, its heads, the cursor and every cell (3DO models freed through 0x45AAA0 and made through 0x45A8D0, a cell's unit slots only when 0 or naming a live unit; in a takeover of a recording that holds its cells, every live unit's +0x110 bits 26 and 27 from the image too, the unit a slot of the array holding a live unit of the image's type), clears the effects (tagpu_patches.c), writes every declared table that pairs with this build's declaration by name, stride, class, key, flags and layout hash within the rows the host holds now, an address only from an ID within its table (the air pool's lock held over the writes); in a round trip (`roundtrip <name> at <T>`, at the frame visit, on a puppet only) it first puts every static SIM_TRANSIENT declaration of the sim files back to its bytes saved at the playing's first frame visit (on the game thread, writing only bytes that differ; the ghost queue s_ghostQ/s_ghostN without s_ghostLock, since in play only the game thread pumps, tagpu_patches.c), and inside that table write fills every byte of every recorded declaration outside its layout's keep with 0xA5 and puts back from its copy every byte the write left at 0xA5, all before the lock is left; then the globals, the pool and the explored map as at a capture point, the viewed seat's temporary sight records made again through the engine's add 0x482910 from the recorded deaths before T (at most the 20 it allows), V's sight with the minimap's fog composite 0x466C20, runs the viewed record's radar pass 0x467440 once, as its 30-tick pass does, then the expiry 0x482130, the seats' and V's 30-tick deadlines and DisplayTimers and every record's DisplayTimer, the notices' and the chat lines' resets 0x47EE30 and 0x463C80, and the pacing stamps. At the replay HUD's request (tagpu_hudinput.c), on the game thread (its clicks in the shield's mouse paths and the wndproc TA pumps on that thread, the packet's fill for the timeline drag's seek, the DrawGameScreen observer for Leave's pause, put back once its question is gone or when it does not open): the pause bit, bit 0 of the word main+0x38A51, read-modify-written as the Pause key does; the game speed's ceiling main+0x38A4B and live speed main+0x38A4D, set by the HUD's speed button, only the presets 5, 10, 20, 40 and 80 and only while the player's speed is saved; the ceiling is exchanged with the player's around each settings save and both put back at the teardown; and seeks, queued for the frame visit as the trigger's are. The byte-matched before-only observer at 0x49692D, after the scheduler call 0x496928 and before the count test 0x496932 and step call 0x49693E, in a test launch whose trigger said `turbo on` (and not `turbo off` since) first writes the due-tick count main+0x38A3B to the k of 1..5 tagpu_turbo.on gave at attach, or to 0 while the recorder's ring holds more than half its size (unless bit 1 of main+0x38A51, the scheduler's lag scale, is set), on any level in play outside rewind, then reads GameTime and bounds that count within the scheduler's 0..5 by the nearest upcoming T-1 in the 24-entry armed round-trip table; the bound only reduces it, on a playing puppet outside rewind, on the same GAME thread that arms and consumes the targets, before the step caches its count in EBX; and on any level in play outside rewind it bounds the count by an armed `pause at <T>` the same way, whose frame visit at T sets bit 0 of main+0x38A51 as the Pause key does (`resume` clears it); and in a test launch whose tagpu_hold.on was present at attach (the launch's hold, test launches only), it bounds the count to 1 from the level's first batch until the hold is taken, and the frame visit after that batch's step sets bit 0 of main+0x38A51 the same way. The byte-matched observer at 0x496920, before the pause test, on the GAME thread, sets that bit again while rewind owns the level or while a pause the trigger or the launch's hold took stands (until `resume`), so no step runs between. An unavailable guard refuses round trips, the turbo, the pause and the hold. The recording is read by tagpu_recreader.c, which reads no engine memory and is not listed. And the step's phases: from the stubs at 0x495513 and 0x495539 on the GAME thread, the feature calls the recording made in the projectile tick and after it, the tick's input cells, the cells' unit slots put back and the step's feature images, each as at the replay step, from a copy of the step's brackets. And Follow and Jump: at the frame visit, on the GAME thread, the 1x view main+0x37E37/+0x37E3B read to fit the recorded camera to this screen (tagpu_persp.c, engine-free), the eye handed to tagpu_zoom_follow, which writes the camera; nothing of the camera is written here. And the viewer's record: on a level that views record 10, once a GameTime at the frame visit on the GAME thread, the players phase's work for the viewed record that its loop over records 0-9 never reaches, the minimap rebuild 0x466DC0 and, when record 10's 30-tick deadline +0xF0 is due, that deadline and the radar pass 0x467440 and the minimap's fog composite 0x466C20, all between the record's buffers' allocation (the level init's call, or a live rewind's 0x464700) and the teardown wrap's entry, which ends the puppet mode first. And the overlays: at the frame visit, on the GAME thread, the recorded cursors, clicks and selections built from the reader's world (in a multiplayer recording with the composed units and the owners' contributed perspectives) and GameTime alone, each recorded seat's colour taken once a playing through its own lookup -- the engine's colour sequence *(main+0x148DB), the logo bounded by its count, one frame of at most 64x64 decoded -- at the frame visit or in the packet's fill (tagpu_replay_players), and handed to the publisher by value after the draw. And for the replay HUD's stats window, on the GAME thread before its walker starts (tagpu_hudmarks.c), tagpu_replay_army_values copies each unit type's army value out of the level's unit defs (main+0x1439B, the count main+0x1438F, stride 0x249: the metal cost +0x18A, the mover byte +0x22F, the tags +0x245), which a load makes and only the teardown frees. And the pause icon: a stub on DrawGameScreen's pause test 0x46A107, on the GAME thread inside the draw, tests the pause bit of main+0x38A51 as the engine does and skips the igpaused draw while a HUD control carries the pause (tagpu_hudinput_on_screen); it writes nothing. And the message lines: a stub on DrawChatText's store of its first line's top (0x4640D6..0x4640DD), on the GAME thread inside the draw, stores the value tagpu_hudinput.c keeps on the same thread instead of 0x34 and reads nothing of the engine's. And the draw's unit list: at the end of a frame visit that ran a seek, changed the viewed record or moved the eye, on the GAME thread, the cull 0x48BAE0, which rewrites the list *(main+0x1435F) and main+0x14367 from the unit array, eye, rect and viewed record, as its call at 0x49697B does. And the script slices, on the GAME thread. A plan from the step's events at the capture point. A gate inside the unit tick that names the unit by arithmetic against the level's array. DoScriptsNow 0x4B0D60 at a marker, a create's end or the step's end, on the +0x9A COB object of a unit live in its slot, which only that unit's destructor frees (0x486D8A), on the same thread. And the owner's queries, on the GAME thread at their place in the step's events: 0x4B0BC0 on the same +0x9A COB object of a unit live in its slot, under the same lifetime. And Teleport's moves, on the GAME thread at their place in the step's events: the flare 0x471FD0 on two points of the replay's own (it copies them), through the recorder, and the set-position 0x48A9F0 on a unit live in its slot.
src/tagpu_scenario.c     tooling             presentation  # both halves on the game thread: the applier on the tick detour, detection on the flip
src/tagpu_cat.c          tooling             presentation  # unit/feature catalogues behind tagpu_units.trigger / tagpu_features.trigger
src/tagpu_gui_snap.c     tooling             presentation  # the gadget-tree snapshot behind tagpu_ui.trigger
src/tagpu_zoom.c         publisher           presentation  # the camera clamp 0x41C3C0, the three target-clamp stubs 0x41C808/0x41C93B (the centre-ons' smooth arms) and 0x41CAF7 (the follow, in the stepper), the GetTPosition guard 0x498EF9 inside the mouse->world 0x498DA0, the debug-overlay call 0x468DBA inside DrawGameScreen, the minimap-rect and ScrollSpeed-save redirects, the command apply at the top of every in-play draw, the scenario camera's hand-over from the flip observer (written by the next apply, or on the spot where no apply runs), and the replay's Follow and Jump handed over by its frame visit before the draw call 0x4969CD (the eye and the scroll target written by the next apply, or on the spot where no apply runs, with the engine's follow released on an epoch's first write): every engine word here is read and written on the game thread; the render half (the levers, the follow lever, the anchor arithmetic, the predicted eye) reads only the packet
src/tagpu_vpwide.c       publisher           presentation  # the 0x498DA0 mouse->world redirect and the clip guard inside DrawGameScreen; the rect is widened and restored from the command apply, game thread; the true rect is read on the game thread only
src/tagpu_native.c       fenced              presentation  # the unit array, the poses, the option bits and the ground heights all come from the packet; what is left is per-LEVEL assets under tagpu_reclaim's teardown fence — the model templates behind MODEL_PTRS (whose base and bound the packet carries). Both fog grids come out of the packet. tagpu_native_owns_unit/owns_obj read the unit record and its def on the GAME thread, for the publisher and the owndraw classifier
src/tagpu_scaffold.c     fenced              presentation  # the units, the feature anchors, the map and sweep dimensions all come from the packet; the FeatureDef records and the GAF frames it stamps from are per-MAP assets under the fence
src/tagpu_render3do.c    fenced              presentation  # there is no write-back, and the shade table and build-state formulas take the packet's copies; the face material helpers still read the model template's face records and their GAF frames, and the atlas decodes the GAF frame of an effects model's face at the address the packet carries when its texels are not painted yet (tagpu_r3d_atlas_texels), per level, under the fence; its wiki instance (tagpu_scene.h) reads only the face records and GAF frames of the wiki's arena, immutable and never freed
src/tagpu_feat.c         fenced              presentation  # the anchors, their six height bytes, the map dimensions and the player and option bytes come from the packet; the FeatureDef records and the wreck records it draws GAF frames out of are per-MAP assets under the fence
src/tagpu_text.c         pure-engine-code    presentation  # the glyph blitter 0x4CCF60..0x4CD00E over ONE-GLYPH FONT OBJECTS OF OURS: it reads its arguments and writes its destination. Both text paths hand it a copy — the marker font's glyphs come in the frame packet and the UI's come as bits in the string op — so this file dereferences no engine font and carries no probe
src/tagpu_terr.c         fenced              presentation  # the tile set, the tile map and the height byte of every cell, per map under the fence; the level packet decides its fate
src/tagpu_posebake.c     fenced              presentation  # the per-type geometry bake walks the model template tree on the render thread; its wiki instance (tagpu_scene.h) walks only the trees of the wiki's arena, immutable and never freed
src/tagpu_gaf.c          session-reader      presentation  # GAF banks and the cursor table: loaded once, never rewritten. The "fx" bank the effects and particle passes draw from is one of them [VERIFIED 2026-09-12]: 0x429870 loads it and resolves every named sequence into main+0x147AB.., and its only caller is 0x49134D inside 0x491200, whose only caller is WinMain's 0x49EA62 — so those sequences are a SESSION asset, not a per-level one. This file also reads the frame HEADER for its callers (tagpu_gaf_frame_geom / _subframe), so a pass that only places a sprite dereferences nothing of its own

src/tagpu_recmp_chat_inbox.c publisher        presentation  # copied loading/play chat handoff: callers supply a bounded delivered packet and the process-lifetime main+0x38A47 clock field; the inbox reads that one value inside copied-data ownership, retains no engine pointer, and never calls an engine/SDK function, logs, allocates or acquires another lock while held. One fixed 4096-observation allocation lives until process exit after installation; start and first-producer claim publish copied match/host metadata, and frozen-roster bounds precede transfer into the game-owned text queue.

src/tagpu_resource.c publisher presentation # resource input UI: hooks in BuildCursor and left/right command dispatch, raw mouse capture after the viewport repair, packet-only ghosts. The game thread owns each live level array, definition yard data and unit order chain until teardown; IDs are bounded against their engine counts, grid arithmetic against dimensions and explored-map allocation. Quick Build's candidates are each constructor's build menu, read on the game thread as the page loader 0x41ACE0 reads it: the engine's own file calls (0x4290F0, 0x4BBC40, 0x4BB5B0, 0x4BBD00, 0x4BB710, 0x4BB7C0, 0x4BB5D0) with paths bounded to their buffers and a file to 1 MiB, the download records below [main+0x391C7] and five entries a record, the watched player's side below min([main+0x37F39], 5), 0x488B10's answer below the def count; the menu table holds type numbers only, per level generation. UI scratch is never read by simulation. Accepted commands enter normal reclaim/build/Guard order roots, which the recorder already captures; puppet seats refuse input. Placement query scratch is saved/restored around previews.
src/tagpu_place.c publisher presentation # multi-site build placement UI: an after-observer on the build cursor 0x4197D0, chained last after the resource module's, and a byte-matched stub on the click routing 0x4993BF, both on the game thread inside the in-play mouse handler 0x499200, written at attach. The game thread owns each live level array, definition footprint and unit order chain until teardown; the unit array, the player's block, unit slots and def indices are bounded against their engine counts, every order-list walk is bounded and owner-checked, and no node pointer survives a root call. Sites are judged by the target of the engine's own call at 0x4198C6 and the cursor's altitude calls, with the site test's scratch 0x51E684/0x51E688 saved and restored. A commit enters the engine's order roots 0x439F80 and 0x43ADC0 where the stock click calls its own, which the recorder already captures, and sets main+0x2CC6 bit 5 as 0x498FB4 does; puppet seats refuse every hook. Alt+Z / Alt+X reach tagpu_place_key from the window procedure (tagpu_shield.c), on the game thread, which reads the build cursor's context through the same bounded path; the spacing table is game-thread state handed to the settings store without waiting on its lock. UI scratch is never read by simulation; the packet fill and the GDI square copy values out of it.
